### Current behaviour:

I thought this was originally due to white-listing cookies, but after removing that from my test suite I can see now that the way Cypress sets cookies from post requests is inherently flawed.

~~Doing a POST request which returns a cookie only sets this cookie once the command has run twice. This behaviour is exhibited when retrying the test suite (all tests now pass) or can be worked around, in some (but not all cases), by visiting a page before doing the login command.~~

After further debugging, it is clear that this bug is actually about where the log in request is being made. When logging in at the beginning of each individual `it()` block and running the test inside the callback of the login, the tests pass as expected.

If placing the login command inside a `beforeEach()` block, the test fails, with the cookie being rejected by our server. This would suggest that the `it()` blocks inside each `describe()` block are actually new sessions, making logging in inside a `beforeEach()` block redundant (and counter intuitive).

### Desired behaviour:

Cypress allows the session to login inside a `beforeEach()` block so that the code can be kept DRY.

### Steps to reproduce:

1. Write a custom command which sends a POST request to log you in to your app/service
2. Inside a test file, inside the beforeEach block, execute the custom command
3. Try accessing an authenticated page inside an `it()` block or sending a request

```js
let authToken = ''

describe('/meters', () => {
  // this should work, but Cypress cookie handling is bugged, see:
  // https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/issues/2515
  // beforeEach( () => {
  //   cy.login('mgm')
  // })

  it('retrieves booked meters as an account manager', () => {
    // this works, but now I have to login inside every single it() block
    cy.login('mgm', (response) => { 
      const options = {
        filter: ['parent_id', 'null'],
        fields: ['real-properties', 'id,name,installation-complete'],
        include: 'booked-meters,booked-meter-modules'
      }

      cy.api('GET', 'users', options, authToken).then( (response) => {
        cy.log(response)
      })
    })
  })
})
```

Ideally, for sending authenticated API requests (as seen in the above example) - we would need only to login during the beforeEach block and not need to wrap our api tests inside a login callback every time.

Keep up the great work!

