We have a [Single Page Application](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single-page_application) with a C# server sending mostly empty HTML pages and answering to REST requests. We use Cypress for UI tests, Component tests and E2E tests. The first 2 kinds of tests require a mock-server that we quickly implemented in nodejs to return mostly empty HTML pages and server static files (e.g., CSS and pictures). E2E tests are executed against the real C# server.

To make launching our tests simple on developer's computers, I wrote a script that starts a mock-server and a proxy to the real server, save the 2 network ports in 2 environment variables and then starts Cypress. When launched, Cypress looks at the filename of the test file (e.g., "..._spec.ui.js" or "..._spec.e2e.js") to decide which environment variable to use as `baseUrl`. This way, we have 1 Cypress window open and we can launch either UI or E2E tests: we don't have to restart Cypress with a different `baseUrl`!

