### Current behavior

If I have a test suite with the first test marked with `it.skip()` the `before()` hook is not executed regardless of the number of tests present in a suite.
Similarly, If I have a test suite with the end test marked with `it.skip()` the `after()` hook is not executed regardless of the number of tests present in a suite.

So if I have a test suite with both `before()` and `after()` hooks and with 2 tests only one of which is marked as `it.skip()` I can't get both `before()` and `after()` to execute regardless of how I position the tests inside the suite.

### Desired behavior

`before()` and `after()` hooks are always run if present as long as you have at least one active (not `it.skip()`) test in a suite.

The same logic applies to `describe` blocks instead of suites.

### Test code to reproduce

Example 1:
```
describe('test-suite-with-hooks', (): void => {
    before((): void => {
      cy.log('beforeHook');
    });

    after((): void => {
      cy.log('afterHook');
    });

    it.skip('test1', (): void => {
       cy.log('test1');
